  "narrative": "Critical appraisal: none of R1-R12 has a 95% Welch interval that excludes zero, so there is no discovery evidence sufficient to accept any of the pre-registered positive or negative associations. I therefore avoid claiming any effect in the supplied dataset. Using the observed outcome mean magnitude as an approximate clinical scale benchmark, the approximate 10% differences are about 0.0415 for outcome_001, 0.0175 for outcome_006, 0.0093 for outcome_014, 0.0301 for outcome_011, and 0.0282 for outcome_008. H1 and H4 are the cleanest null-equivalence results: for outcome_001, both exposure contrasts have intervals entirely inside the approximate +/-0.0415 clinical threshold, so I reject clinically meaningful differences for feature_034 and feature_002 on outcome_001. H8 is close to equivalence but is borderline because the lower limit is nearly at the outcome_008 threshold; given ambiguity in the exact comparator-relative benchmark and multiplicity, I leave it unresolved rather than assert equivalence. H3 and H7 have near-zero point estimates but outcome_014 has a small mean magnitude, so the clinical threshold is small and the intervals remain too wide to rule out clinically meaningful differences. H2 and H5 for outcome_006 also have intervals extending beyond the small clinical threshold, and H6 extends into a clinically meaningful positive direction. The interaction estimates are even more uncertain because they use median splits and four-cell contrasts. H10 is the most visually interesting discovery result: a signed interaction of -0.0432 is large relative to outcome_006 mean magnitude, but its interval -0.113 to +0.027 includes both null and clinically meaningful opposite-direction differences, and it is the largest among many exploratory subgroup contrasts. This is not compelling enough to validate. Overall, the evidence pattern is mostly null or imprecise, with no coherent strong effect modification signal. I request no independent validation now because the validation budget is better used later for a precise or replicated claim. The proposed follow-ups test two additional balanced binary predictors and convert the largest interaction lead into a direct subgroup mean effect for H10 while keeping the interpretation exploratory. No causal or generalizability claims are made.",
  "research_notes": "Iteration 1 appraisal state: R1-R12 all include null. Accepted/rejected only for strong clinical-equivalence cases. H1/H4 rejected as clinically null for outcome_001 with feature_034/feature_002. H8 left unresolved as borderline equivalence. Main-effect and interaction uncertainty is dominated by wide CIs, especially for outcome_014 where the 10% clinical benchmark is small. H10 remains the most active lead because it has the largest clinically meaningful-looking signed interaction, but validation is not requested because the interval is too broad and includes the opposite sign after multiplicity. Next registered proposals: screen feature_045 on outcome_014/outcome_001 and feature_065 on outcome_011; also test the high feature_051 direct subgroup effect related to H10. Avoid validation until a claim has both clinically meaningful point estimate and much narrower interval, or unless a scheduled validation arrives. Continue to treat all findings as associations in this dataset only."
}

